WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
I am ready, but please hide the answers

00:00:02.457 --> 00:00:03.021
first.

00:00:03.832 --> 00:00:04.553
What changes

00:00:04.556 --> 00:00:08.122
first during inhalation, air
pressure or muscle action?

00:00:08.864 --> 00:00:09.717
Muscle action.

00:00:09.996 --> 00:00:11.858
Diaphragm contracts and flattens.

00:00:12.062 --> 00:00:13.317
Ribs move up and out.

00:00:13.526 --> 00:00:14.638
Volume increases.

00:00:14.739 --> 00:00:15.612
Pressure falls.

00:00:15.832 --> 00:00:16.672
Air enters.

00:00:17.444 --> 00:00:17.658
How

00:00:17.658 --> 00:00:17.857
does

00:00:17.878 --> 00:00:20.436
oxygen cross from an alveolus into blood?

00:00:21.172 --> 00:00:24.626
By diffusion down its
gradient across thin walls.

00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:26.951
It is not actively pumped through.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.184
Why are many alveoli better
than a few large spaces?

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:34.478
More total surface area for gas exchange.

00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:37.222
And why is respiration
different from breathing?

00:00:37.763 --> 00:00:40.684
Respiration is the cell
chemistry that releases energy.

00:00:41.094 --> 00:00:42.569
Breathing is ventilation.

00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:43.958
Excellent.

00:00:44.284 --> 00:00:46.695
You have rescued every imaginary mark.

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:52.458
One system, three jobs: move the air,
exchange the gases, supply the cells
